{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791571149,"nonce":"vflIC8VTW5sJiR75cqIdj0dcH9c=","title":"Subscribing and disposing N subscribers of one signal is O(N^2) in the reactive graph","message":"Reported by the ashui session: disposing 100,000 computeds that all read one signal, at a tree flush, was still running after 14 minutes, every sample in ReactiveGraph::dispose_derived inside SmallVec\u003cSubscriberId\u003e::retain and SubscriberId::eq. A UI's bindings commonly share a signal (theme, selection, hover target), so unmounting a large list goes quadratic in its size.\n\nReproduced, N deriveds reading one signal, timed on the graph directly. Doubling N quadruples both:\n\n    N=5,000    subscribe 7 ms     dispose 51 ms\n    N=10,000   subscribe 26 ms    dispose 208 ms\n    N=20,000   subscribe 102 ms   dispose 843 ms\n\nAt 100,000 the same logic took 28 s to dispose here (measured, with eager removal restored). Subscribing is quadratic too, which the report did not mention: each subscription checked subscribers.contains first, a pass over the whole list.\n\nCause: every removal from a signal's subscriber list was SmallVec::retain over the whole list, in dispose_derived, dispose_effect and on every re-run (get_derived and resubscribe_effect each unsubscribed from everything and resubscribed, retain plus contains per dependency). So a change to a shared signal re-running N subscribers also cost O(N^2).\n\nFix, and why this one:\n- Dispose is lazy. Notification already ignores a subscriber id whose node is gone (mark_dirty looks it up and returns), so a stale id in a list is harmless. Disposal now only counts the departure, and the list is compacted when half of it is stale: linear in total. A list of 16 or fewer is still edited at once, so small cases behave exactly as before.\n- A re-run touches only the dependencies that changed. Both lists are sorted and deduplicated, so a re-run that reads what it read before changes nothing, keeps its place in each list and does no scan. This removes the contains check as well.\n- due_host_effects: dispose_effect no longer retains over it (take_due_host_effects already drops ids that are gone), and flush no longer scans it with contains; duplicates are removed once, at take.\n\nRejected: swap-remove with back-pointers is O(1) but reorders a signal's subscribers, and that order is the order its effects run in, so it changes observable behaviour. A hash set per signal also loses the order and costs memory for every signal, long list or not.\n\nMeasured after the fix, same probe: N=20,000 subscribe 2 ms, dispose 0.8 ms; doubling N doubles both.\n\nTests (reactive::subscribers): 100,000 subscribers subscribe and dispose inside an 8 s bound (28 s with eager removal restored); a long list is compacted as subscribers leave; a disposed effect is never notified and survivors keep their order through compaction; a re-run keeps its place; a changed dependency moves the subscription; reading a signal twice subscribes once; a host effect that became due twice is handed out once. Eager removal fails the first, no compaction fails two.","files":null}]}